-- title:
--   Unique transversal branch over t in a normal finite cover
-- statement:
--   Let $A'$ be a Noetherian integrally closed domain with fraction field $K'$, and let $B$ be an integrally closed domain that is an $A'$-algebra, module-finite over $A'$ and with faithful $A'$-action, with fraction field $L'$; assume $L'$ is an extension of $K'$, separable over $K'$, compatibly with the two towers $A' \to K' \to L'$ and $A' \to B \to L'$. Let $\varpi, t \in A'$ be such that the principal ideal $(t)$ is prime and $A'/(t)$ has characteristic zero, and assume the fibre ring $B/\varpi B$ is a Dedekind domain. Let $y \subseteq B$ be a maximal ideal whose contraction to $A'$ is the ideal $(\varpi, t)$, and assume that the localisation of $A'$ at that contracted prime is a regular local ring whose Krull dimension is exactly $2$. Assume finally that $A' \to B$ is unramified at every prime $Q \subseteq y$ of $B$ of height $1$ with $t \notin Q$ (in the sense of `Algebra.IsUnramifiedAt`). Then there is a prime ideal $\mathfrak Q \subseteq y$ of $B$ with $t \in \mathfrak Q$ and $\varpi \notin \mathfrak Q$ such that, for every local ring $\mathcal O$ which is a $B$-algebra realising the localisation of $B$ at $y$, the ideal generated by the image of $\mathfrak Q$ together with the image of $\varpi$ is the whole maximal ideal of $\mathcal O$; and $\mathfrak Q$ is the only prime of $B$ contained in $y$ that contains $t$ and avoids $\varpi$.
--
--   This is the commutative-algebra core of the statement that a finite normal cover of a regular two-dimensional local germ with regular parameters $(\varpi, t)$, unramified away from $\{t = 0\}$ and with Dedekind special fibre $\{\varpi = 0\}$, meets $\{t = 0\}$ in a single branch through each point of the cover, and that this branch crosses $\{\varpi = 0\}$ transversally. It is used in the analysis of charts on modular curves of full level and of $\Gamma_0$-type, where $t$ cuts out the locus in question and $\varpi$ the special fibre; the proof reduces to the case of a local base, using freeness of $B$ over the regular two-dimensional local ring (Auslander–Buchsbaum).
